---
import ProfileToc from '../../components/ProfileToc.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { release } from '../../data/release';
import { publicEntries, formatDate } from '../../utils/content';

const actors:any[] = await publicEntries('actors');
const campaigns:any[] = await publicEntries('campaigns');
const malware:any[] = await publicEntries('malware');
const tools:any[] = await publicEntries('tools');
const sources:any[] = await publicEntries('sources');
const updates:any[] = await publicEntries('updates');
const aliases = actors.reduce((sum, actor:any) => sum + actor.data.aliases.length, 0);
const latest = [...updates].sort((left:any, right:any) => {
  const byDate = +new Date(right.data.date) - +new Date(left.data.date);
  return byDate || left.data.slug.localeCompare(right.data.slug);
})[0];
const stats = [
  ['Actors', actors.length],
  ['Aliases', aliases],
  ['Campaigns', campaigns.length],
  ['Software', malware.length + tools.length],
  ['References', sources.length],
  ['Changes', updates.length]
];
const sections = [
  { slug: 'what-it-is', text: 'What APT Notes is' },
  { slug: 'audience-and-scope', text: 'Audience and scope' },
  { slug: 'selection', text: 'Selection and coverage' },
  { slug: 'sources-and-attribution', text: 'Sources and attribution' },
  { slug: 'editorial-workflow', text: 'Editorial workflow' },
  { slug: 'review-policy', text: 'Review policy' },
  { slug: 'corrections-and-versions', text: 'Corrections and versions' },
  { slug: 'machine-access', text: 'Machine access' },
  { slug: 'limitations', text: 'Known limitations' },
  { slug: 'maintainer', text: 'Maintainer and contact' }
];
---
<BaseLayout title="About" description="Purpose, audience, ownership, maintenance and limitations of APT Notes by HECAVEX.">
  <article class="shell about-shell profile-shell">
    <header class="about-head">
      <div class="about-summary">
        <p class="eyebrow">APT Notes / About</p>
        <h1>A small catalogue with an explicit evidence boundary.</h1>
        <p class="lead">APT Notes publishes selected threat-actor dossiers and the campaigns, software, techniques and references needed to inspect their claims.</p>
        <p>It is designed for readers who need to know not only which names are connected, but which publisher made the claim, what the evidence supports and where uncertainty remains.</p>
        <div class="about-actions"><a class="button" href="/actors/">Browse actor dossiers →</a><a href="/knowledge/">Search Knowledge →</a><a href="/about/methodology/">Read methodology →</a></div>
      </div>

      <dl class="project-facts" aria-label="APT Notes project status">
        <div><dt>Primary product</dt><dd>Deep actor dossiers; supporting records open in context and remain directly addressable.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Publication model</dt><dd>English-only, public, static and readable without an account.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Owner and editor</dt><dd><a href="https://hecavex.com/en/about/">Deividas Lis / HECAVEX</a></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Maintenance</dt><dd><span class="badge current">Maintained</span> Best effort; no monitoring, notification or response SLA.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Last substantive change</dt><dd>{latest ? <><time datetime={latest.data.date.toISOString()}>{formatDate(latest.data.date)}</time><br /><a href={`/changes/#${latest.data.slug}`}>{latest.data.title}</a></> : 'No public changes yet'}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Current data release</dt><dd><span class="mono">{release.datasetVersion}</span><br /><a href="/api/index.json">Data catalogue</a> · <a href="/api/version.json">Version manifest</a></dd></div>
      </dl>
    </header>

    <div class="stat-grid about-stats" aria-label="Public APT Notes catalogue statistics">
      {stats.map(([name, count]) => <div class="stat"><strong>{count}</strong><span>{name}</span></div>)}
    </div>

    <div class="profile-grid about-grid">
      <ProfileToc items={sections} contextLabel="On this page" title="About APT Notes" ariaLabel="About APT Notes sections" />
      <div class="prose profile-body about-body">
        <section id="what-it-is">
          <h2>What APT Notes is</h2>
          <p>APT Notes is a source-backed intelligence publication organised around selected threat actors. Actor dossiers are the deepest editorial records. Campaigns, malware, tools, ATT&amp;CK techniques and references provide the evidence and operational context needed to read those dossiers critically.</p>
          <p>The catalogue preserves source-specific naming and attribution instead of flattening every overlapping label into one asserted identity. A relationship is published as sourced evidence only when a dossier records a concrete procedure and at least one supporting reference.</p>
          <p>Long-form investigations and commentary remain on <a href="https://hecavex.com/en/research/">HECAVEX Research ↗</a>. APT Notes is the structured, reusable catalogue behind selected parts of that work.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="audience-and-scope">
          <h2>Audience and scope</h2>
          <p>The publication is written for threat-intelligence analysts, defenders, incident responders, investigators, journalists and researchers who need an inspectable route from an actor name to underlying public evidence.</p>
          <p>In scope are actor identity, source-specific attribution, aliases, campaigns, software, techniques, targeting, vulnerabilities, operational chronology and relevant public references. APT Notes does not host malware, victim data, credentials, private telemetry or live indicators.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="selection">
          <h2>Selection and coverage</h2>
          <p>Coverage expands one dossier at a time. Priority is given to actors relevant to European and Baltic security, actors connected to current HECAVEX research, and records where naming, attribution or cluster boundaries benefit from careful source separation.</p>
          <p>Record count is not used as a maturity signal. APT Notes deliberately remains smaller than broad adversary directories so each actor dossier can retain useful context, evidence and review history.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="sources-and-attribution">
          <h2>Sources and attribution</h2>
          <p>Original publisher links remain primary. Publication date and access date are kept separate, archived links can be added without silently replacing the original, and link health is reported as its own state.</p>
          <p>Government attribution, vendor tracking clusters, legal claims and APT Notes assessments are not interchangeable. Confidence applies to the stated record or relationship; it is not a universal score for everything associated with the actor.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="editorial-workflow">
          <h2>Editorial workflow</h2>
          <p>A public record is assembled from identifiable sources, normalized into the catalogue, checked for identity collisions and reviewed for claim boundaries. Draft records are excluded from the site and every machine-readable export.</p>
          <p>New relationships require an explicit source entity, target entity, evidence statement, confidence level and supporting reference. Broader arrays are shown as catalogue associations until that provenance exists.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="review-policy">
          <h2>Review and staleness policy</h2>
          <p>Actor and supporting records carry separate created, modified and last-reviewed dates. Actor profiles are intended to receive a substantive review at least annually. After 180 days the interface marks review due; after 365 days it marks the record stale.</p>
          <p>A stale label describes editorial review age. It does not mean the actor is inactive, the evidence is false or the linked infrastructure remains operational.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="corrections-and-versions">
          <h2>Corrections, deprecation and versions</h2>
          <p>Material publications, corrections, confidence changes and relationship changes appear in <a href="/changes/">Changes</a> with a stable event ID, affected fields, version transition and an explanation of what changed and why.</p>
          <p>Deprecated, revoked or superseded records remain addressable so citations do not silently break. Their state is exposed in both the human-readable record and JSON output.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="machine-access">
          <h2>Machine-readable access and licensing</h2>
          <p>Every public entity type, the sourced relationship layer and the change record are available as deterministic JSON. Canonical CSV exports and a dedicated Changes Atom feed are published alongside a release manifest.</p>
          <p>Start with the <a href="/api/index.json">data catalogue</a>, inspect the <a href="/api/version.json">release manifest</a>, or use the JSON action on a specific record. The <a href="/licence/">licence and attribution boundary</a> applies to reuse.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="limitations">
          <h2>Known limitations</h2>
          <p class="callout">APT Notes is not a live threat feed, detection service, SIEM, vulnerability scanner, authoritative attribution body or replacement for MITRE ATT&amp;CK.</p>
          <p>Coverage is selective, public reporting can be incomplete and actor boundaries can change. A record describes evidence available at its review date. It does not claim exhaustive visibility, current compromise or operational completeness.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="maintainer">
          <h2>Maintainer and contact</h2>
          <p>APT Notes is researched, edited and maintained by <a href="https://hecavex.com/en/about/">Deividas Lis</a> as part of HECAVEX. Maintenance is best effort and carries no monitoring, takedown, notification or response service level.</p>
          <p>Use the <a href="https://hecavex.com/en/contact/">editorial contact route</a> for factual corrections and the <a href="/security/">security policy</a> for security issues. Sensitive evidence should not be sent through ordinary email.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="about-next" aria-labelledby="about-next-title">
      <div class="section-head"><h2 id="about-next-title">Continue into the evidence</h2></div>
      <div class="grid">
        <a class="panel" href="/actors/"><strong>Browse dossiers</strong><br /><span>Open actor records →</span></a>
        <a class="panel" href="/knowledge/"><strong>Inspect supporting records</strong><br /><span>Search Knowledge →</span></a>
        <a class="panel" href="/relationships/"><strong>Review sourced edges</strong><br /><span>Open Relationships →</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
